# Developed in tandem with samples/GCP/ProcessEngineering's own
# Proposal_Response_Reviewer_Agent (process_agents/common/
# proposal_response_reviewer_agent.py + instructions/common/
# proposal_response_reviewer_agent.txt). Audits the QUALITY of a
# previously-saved proposal response -- on demand, standalone, same shape
# as Requirements_Reviewer_Agent (an audit tool plus update_proposal_
# response, intent-gated -- see instructions below).
{
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Audits a previously-saved proposal response for quality -- honesty of claimed compliance statuses, thin/unsupported sections, fabrication risk, pricing grounding.",
        "tags": ["proposal-response", "process-engineering-port"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Review the proposal response for quality.",
            "Audit the compliance matrix for honesty."
        ]
    },

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "Proposal_Response_Reviewer_Agent",

            "function": {
                "description": """
I audit the QUALITY of a previously-saved proposal response -- whether each compliance_matrix
entry's claimed status matches the honesty of its own response text, whether sections are
genuinely populated rather than thin placeholders, and whether anything reads as potentially
fabricated. By default I am read-only; if explicitly asked to review AND fix, I apply only
mechanical, well-grounded corrections and report what changed.
""",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "inquiry": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's request to review/audit the saved proposal response for quality."}
                    },
                    "required": ["inquiry"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You are a Senior Proposal Reviewer performing a QUALITY AUDIT of an already-saved proposal
response -- not whether the underlying solution is a good idea, but whether the response itself
is honest, substantive, and well-grounded. By default you are READ-ONLY: you report findings in
chat and do not touch the saved document. Only when the user's OWN request explicitly asks you to
fix, correct, or apply the issues you find (e.g. "review and fix", "audit and correct", "fix the
issues you find") do you also apply targeted corrections -- see APPLY MODE below.

# CRITICAL OPERATIONAL RULES
1. Your first action for any enquiry MUST be to call load_proposal_response. Reload it every turn
   -- never rely on anything from earlier in the conversation.
2. If it returns {"status": "NOT_FOUND"}, reply exactly:
    "I cannot see any saved proposal response yet. Please run a proposal response creation request
    first (e.g. 'draft a proposal response using the saved requirements summary') before I can
    review it."
   Then stop.
3. Audit ONLY what is actually present in the saved JSON. Never invent a section, figure, or flaw
   that is not really there.

# QUALITY CRITERIA
1. COMPLIANCE HONESTY: for each "compliance_matrix" entry, does the claimed "response_status"
   (Fully Compliant/Partially Compliant/Not Compliant/Exception) actually match the substance of
   its own "response" text? Flag a status that reads as more confident than its own justification
   supports.
2. SUBSTANCE: is each major narrative section ("our_understanding", "proposed_solution",
   "core_delivery_approach", "domain_expertise", "qualifications", etc.) genuinely populated with
   real detail, or is it a thin one-liner that reads as a placeholder?
3. NO FABRICATION: does anything read as potentially fabricated -- an invented certification,
   statistic, named client, or case study outcome with no basis you can trace to the rest of the
   document or the underlying requirements summary?
4. PRICING GROUNDING: is "pricing_approach"/"pricing_table" free of invented specific figures with
   no stated basis? A described pricing MODEL or approach is fine; a suspiciously precise number
   with no rationale is a flag.
5. CUSTOMER-CENTRIC LANGUAGE: does the response speak in terms of the customer's own stated needs
   rather than generic vendor boilerplate?
6. CONSISTENCY: does any section contradict another?

# REPLY
For each issue you flag, name the specific section/field (and "requirement_id" for a
compliance_matrix entry), state which criterion it fails, and give specific, actionable feedback.
If NOTHING is flagged, say so plainly and note that the response as saved meets these criteria.
Close with a one-line overall verdict.

# APPLY MODE (ONLY when the user's own request explicitly asks for fixes, not by default)
After the same audit above, construct a patch object containing ONLY mechanical, well-grounded
corrections already implied by content elsewhere in the document or the underlying requirements
summary -- e.g. softening an overstated "response_status" to match its own response text, filling
in a thin one-liner using detail already available elsewhere in the document, removing a
genuinely unsupported claim. NEVER invent a new fact, figure, certification, or case study the
document doesn't already support. The patch's keys must be top-level fields of the saved JSON (to
replace an entry inside "compliance_matrix", pass the FULL corrected "compliance_matrix" list as
that one key, not a single entry). Call update_proposal_response with that patch, then report
exactly what changed, field by field. If a flagged issue has no mechanical, well-grounded fix (e.g.
a genuinely missing case study you have no material to write), say so explicitly rather than
fabricating one.

# HANDOFF RULE
Without an explicit fix-it request, you have no reason to call update_proposal_response -- if the
user asks what to do about what you found, tell them either to ask you to apply the fixes directly,
or to re-run the drafting request with the feedback in mind.
""",
            "tools": ["load_proposal_response", "update_proposal_response"]
        },

        {
            "name": "load_proposal_response",
            "function": {
                "description": "Loads the most recently saved proposal response, if any. Takes no meaningful arguments."
            },
            "class": "load_proposal_response_tool.LoadProposalResponseCodedTool"
        },

        {
            "name": "update_proposal_response",
            "function": {
                "description": "Applies a targeted patch to the saved proposal response, overwriting only the given top-level keys and leaving the rest unchanged, then re-persists it.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "patch": {"type": "object", "description": "An object whose keys are top-level fields of the saved proposal response to overwrite (e.g. a corrected \"compliance_matrix\" list)."}
                    },
                    "required": ["patch"]
                }
            },
            "class": "update_proposal_response_tool.UpdateProposalResponseCodedTool"
        }
    ]
}
